## Bevy version

0.16.0-rc.2

## Relevant system information

```ignore
`AdapterInfo { name: "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050", vendor: 4318, device: 9479, device_type: DiscreteGpu, driver: "NVIDIA", driver_info: "565.57.01", backend: Vulkan }`
```

## What you did

The only way I've found to get anisotropic filtering is by either altering the ImagePlugin's default_sampler to use an anisotropy_clamp of, say, 16, or by creating a basic asset processor to alter this value for specific assets. This worked well at fixing the blur for non-filtered mipmapped textures when viewed at an incline.

The asset processor writes images as basis-universal when the feature is enabled.

## What went wrong

Changing the anisotropy_clamp seems to no longer have any effect, and textures _generated by CompressedImageSaver_ remain blurry at inclined angles

Png converted to basis-universal via the asset processor (presumably with CompressedImageSaver):

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b4e75fbb-1690-440f-bd61-c89c6cead102)

Compared to a manually generated ktx2 texture:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/975aa39f-e75b-426e-b729-00b68e09c34c)

Edit: ... And a .basis texture generated by [a third-party tool](https://basis.dev.jibencaozuo.com/):

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7f20dc16-fea2-4ed3-bb58-c87e6a9b898b)

## Additional information

~~I'm not sure whether this is due to the way bevy generates basis-universal textures, or how they're loaded and sampled.~~
Edit: third-party .basis textures seem to work just fine.

I haven't found any documentation regarding anisotropic filtering anywhere, so my assumption regarding anisotropy_clamp corresponding to AF levels could be incorrect.

I've tried both altering the default image sampler as well as creating my own asset processor:

```rust
#[derive(Default)]
struct BasicImageTransformer;

impl AssetTransformer for BasicImageTransformer {
    type AssetInput = Image;
    type AssetOutput = Image;
    type Settings = ();
    type Error = Infallible;

    async fn transform<'a>(
        &'a self,
        mut asset: TransformedAsset<Self::AssetInput>,
        _settings: &'a Self::Settings,
    ) -> Result<TransformedAsset<Self::AssetOutput>, Self::Error> {
        asset.sampler = ImageSampler::Descriptor(
            ImageSamplerDescriptor {
                anisotropy_clamp: 16,
                ..ImageSamplerDescriptor::linear()
            }
        );
        Ok(asset)
    }
}
```
